				<article class="draft-prose bg-white p-12 rounded-2xl border border-gray-200">

					<h1>New Vote: Invest in Dead Internet Theory?</h1>

					<h2>The Question</h2>

					<p><strong>Should KmikeyM commit $5,000 from the shareholder fund as the first slug of capital into Paizley Lee's Dead Internet Theory project, in exchange for a revenue share starting from her first paid event, capped at 2x return?</strong></p>

					<h2>Background</h2>

					<p>For eighteen years we have sat on the funds shareholders invest in KmikeyM. We earn modest interest but maintain full liquidity to facilitate any withdrawal of funds. This is a vote to test a more pro-active financial stance, back a new venture, and put a portion of that money to work.</p>

					<p>Paizley Lee (kidgrandma / WORKSUCKS™) is producing a series of in-person gaming events called Dead Internet Theory. The framing is a cyber-café LAN party as a piece of cultural counter-programming: people in a room, on real machines, playing real games together, building things, leaving with physical objects from the night. The thesis she presents is <em>"back to an internet that was a place, not a performance."</em> After an initial play-test of one of her games, I spent fifty-six minutes on a video call with her on April 29 about the new venture. I came out of that call inspired by her vision and how cleanly it maps onto the KmikeyM ethos.</p>

					<p>The thesis-fit with KmikeyM is the part that is most exciting me. I have been describing KmikeyM in private notes for years as "a project that solves loneliness," and Dead Internet Theory is shaped like the same answer: people in a room, participation over performance, the opposite of the algorithmic feed. I did not expect her IRL gaming-events deck to map this cleanly onto the through-line of our own project.</p>

					<h2>Why Now</h2>

					<p>Paizley's LA proof-of-concept is May 17th. The shareholder vote process takes about two weeks from post to close, which puts a clean vote close on or around May 14th. That timing reaches Paizley before her summer Brooklyn dates, where the money actually does its work.</p>

					<p>Her plan is a six-stop arc through 2026 that begins with the LA proof-of-concept on May 17th, moves to Brooklyn in the summer, and lands a longer-form game in New York in September. The first LA event is bare-bones on purpose to create a proof-of-concept. KmikeyM's funds are not enough to fund the whole arc on their own, but with shareholder approval we'd commit as the first money in and become a credibility line she can use to close the rest of the raise.</p>

					<h2>The Proposal</h2>

					<p>If this passes, KmikeyM commits $5,000 from the shareholder fund to Dead Internet Theory under the terms locked in our standing New Vote Terms of Engagement policy. The headline structure:</p>

					<ul>
						<li><strong>$5,000 transferred to Paizley Lee</strong> by approximately May 11th, contingent on this vote passing.</li>
						<li><strong>Two-stage revenue share</strong> beginning at her first paid event, designed so KmikeyM recovers principal quickly and rides along quietly until cap.</li>
						<li><strong>2x return cap.</strong> Once KmikeyM receives $10,000 back, the participation ends and KmikeyM is out clean.</li>
						<li><strong>No equity, no cap-table position, no follow-on obligation.</strong> A single one-time deployment with a defined exit.</li>
						<li><strong>Permission to credit ourselves as a "producer"</strong> of Dead Internet Theory events in our own materials. Paizley is under no obligation to publicly credit KmikeyM.</li>
						<li><strong>Free shareholder entry</strong> to any Dead Internet Theory event, up to 5 people per event.</li>
					</ul>

					<p>The structural shape is closer to a publishing advance than a venture investment: capital out, defined repayment from event revenue, capped return, then done. Final percentages, cadence, and term are set in the private terms sheet and will be summarized in the post that links to this vote.</p>

					<h2>What This Doesn't Change</h2>

					<ul>
						<li><strong>The shareholder fund principal floor.</strong> $5K is sized to be loseable. The fund retains enough reserve to cover redemption pressure regardless of outcome.</li>
						<li><strong>My role in KmikeyM.</strong> I am not becoming an event producer or operationally involved in Dead Internet Theory. I am in Kosovo through early June and cannot run logistics. The deal is capital and remote introductions as needed, not labor.</li>
						<li><strong>KmikeyM's investing posture.</strong> This is not a turn toward KmikeyM as a venture vehicle. If this works, the next investment-shaped vote gets evaluated on its own. If this fails, KmikeyM does not stop being KmikeyM.</li>
						<li><strong>My ownership of share economics.</strong> I am not personally taking any cut, fee, or upside from this deal. Returns go to the shareholder fund.</li>
					</ul>

					<h2>What This Vote Is NOT About</h2>

					<p>This is not a vote about whether Dead Internet Theory is a good cultural project. I think it is. Whether you do is a separate question and not the one in front of you. The vote is about whether KmikeyM should be the entity that backs it.</p>

					<p>This is also not a vote of confidence in my taste. If Dead Internet Theory loses money, that is a real outcome with real loss of fund capital, and it should weigh on a no vote. The thesis fit and my enthusiasm are inputs to the case, not insurance against the downside.</p>

					<h2>The Arguments</h2>

					<h3>For (Yes)</h3>

					<ul>
						<li><strong>Latent capital should work.</strong> The shareholder fund has sat near-static for years, sized for redemption that rarely happens. Putting a small slug of it into a defined-exit, capped-return investment is a more interesting use of the fund than letting it earn nothing.</li>
						<li><strong>Thesis-aligned.</strong> Dead Internet Theory is shaped like the same answer KmikeyM has been giving for eighteen years: people in a room, participation over performance, the messy real thing instead of the algorithmic-feed simulation. Backing it is consistent with our work.</li>
						<li><strong>Defined return path with a hard cap.</strong> This is not patronage and not equity. The 2x cap means the upside is bounded, the downside is bounded, and the exit is defined. Shareholders know exactly what they are buying.</li>
						<li><strong>Producer credit.</strong> KmikeyM earns the right to refer to itself as a producer of these events in our own materials. That credit compounds across our channels even if the financial return underdelivers.</li>
						<li><strong>Press tailwind.</strong> Paizley has press scheduled around her last project. Backing the project before that hits is structurally more useful than backing it after.</li>
						<li><strong>Shareholder access.</strong> Up to 5 shareholders per event get free entry, with reports back to the Discord.</li>
					</ul>

					<h3>Against (No)</h3>

					<ul>
						<li><strong>This is a category change for the fund.</strong> The shareholder fund has never been deployed as risk capital before. Saying yes to this vote sets the precedent that it can be. Some shareholders may consider the fund a redemption guarantee, and while the amount of this investment doesn't jeopardize that, a series of poor investments over time would materially weaken it.</li>
						<li><strong>Real loss is plausible.</strong> Paizley is talented and moving fast, but she is also at the beginning of a major evolution of her previous work. A multi-stop tour where every stop after LA depends on her closing sponsorships and venues she has not yet closed. A loss-of-capital outcome is on the table.</li>
						<li><strong>The return path runs through paid events and through sponsors.</strong> KmikeyM does not see a dollar back until Paizley successfully runs her first paid event later this year. That is a chain of execution risks: LA has to work, the next event has to land, sponsorships have to close, the format has to build in reputation. Any break in the chain delays or kills the return.</li>
						<li><strong>Mike is not in the room.</strong> I am in Kosovo through early June, and Portland in the summer. I cannot help produce an LA event, cannot help negotiate New York venues, cannot do hands-on damage control if a stop falters. KmikeyM's capital is going somewhere I cannot directly support.</li>
						<li><strong>Vote-NO risk.</strong> Once a public vote runs, a NO outcome is a public signal that KmikeyM rejected Paizley's project. That is a worse signal for her than no vote at all. The honest case for voting NO is real, and shareholders should not feel pressured to vote YES to protect Paizley from a public NO.</li>
					</ul>

					<h2>The Honest Tension</h2>

					<p>I want this vote to pass. I should name that, because it is the bias most likely to shape how this is written.</p>

					<p>The thesis-fit between Dead Internet Theory and KmikeyM is the cleanest thesis-fit I have seen on a non-KmikeyM project in years. That excitement is real, and it is also a thing that makes me want help looking at the trade-offs. If someone I didn't know pitched the same project with a less-aligned thesis, I do not know that I would be writing this vote.</p>

					<p>The inner mechanics of the deal (the two-stage revenue-share rates, distribution cadence, term limit, recipient entity) are kept private under our standing New Vote Terms of Engagement policy, the same protocol that has covered every private negotiation KmikeyM has run since 2013. What you are voting on is the public ask: $5,000 from the shareholder fund as a first-money-in commitment, with a revenue share, a 2x hard cap, and producer credit on the events. The inner numbers are mine to negotiate; the headline is yours to ratify.</p>

					<h2>How to Vote</h2>

					<ul>
						<li><strong>YES</strong> — KmikeyM commits $5,000 from the shareholder fund to Dead Internet Theory, with inner deal mechanics structured under New Vote Terms of Engagement.</li>
						<li><strong>NO</strong> — KmikeyM does not deploy capital. The conversation with Paizley remains open for non-financial forms of support (introductions, content cross-promotion, shareholder attendance at events).</li>
					</ul>

					<p>This vote will end on <strong>[DATE — fill in based on post date + 14 days]</strong>.</p>

					<h2>What Happens Next</h2>

					<p><strong>If passed:</strong></p>
					<ol>
						<li>Mike and Paizley settle final terms in a shared document. Open items in the private terms sheet get filled in.</li>
						<li>$5,000 wires from the KmikeyM shareholder fund to Paizley by approximately May 11th.</li>
						<li>KmikeyM gains the right to credit itself as a producer of Dead Internet Theory in our own materials.</li>
						<li>Shareholders get a follow-up post after the LA event (May 17th) with a real read on how the project is performing.</li>
						<li>Revenue-share clock starts at the first paid event.</li>
					</ol>

					<p><strong>If failed:</strong></p>
					<ol>
						<li>KmikeyM does not deploy capital.</li>
						<li>I let Paizley know directly, and we discuss other forms of support.</li>
						<li>This vote does not get re-proposed in this form.</li>
					</ol>

